CAT = "auwhiteHeights"; SEG = "HGT"
Q = [
("Which way of managing a fall risk is highest in the hierarchy?",
 "Doing the work on the ground", ["Using a fall-arrest harness", "Working from a ladder", "Using a work positioning system"],
 "The best fall control is to avoid working at height, for example by doing the work on the ground or from a solid floor. Harnesses and ladders come lower because they still leave a person exposed."),
("A guardrail along an open edge is an example of:",
 "A fall prevention device", ["A fall-arrest system", "An administrative control", "A work positioning system"],
 "Guardrails, scaffolds and work platforms are fall prevention devices: they stop a fall happening. A fall-arrest system only stops a fall once it has started."),
("If workers use a fall-arrest harness, what else must be in place?",
 "An emergency rescue plan", ["A spare harness on site", "A ladder beside the worker", "A sign at the roof edge"],
 "A worker left hanging in a harness can suffer suspension trauma within minutes, so rescue procedures must be set up and practised before the harness is used."),
("At what angle should an extension ladder be set up?",
 "1 out for every 4 up", ["1 out for every 2 up", "1 out for every 8 up", "Flat against the wall"],
 "The 4 to 1 rule puts the base 1 metre out for every 4 metres of height, about 75 degrees. Steeper can tip backwards and flatter can slide out."),
("How far should a ladder extend above the landing it gives access to?",
 "At least 1 metre", ["At least 30 cm", "At least 2 metres", "Level with the edge"],
 "Extending the ladder at least 1 metre above the landing gives a handhold for stepping on and off. The ladder should also be secured top and bottom."),
("While climbing a ladder, you should keep:",
 "Three points of contact", ["Two points of contact", "One hand free for tools", "Your weight leaning back"],
 "Three points of contact means two hands and a foot, or two feet and a hand, on the ladder at all times. Carry tools in a belt or haul them up separately."),
("A scaffolding licence is needed to erect a scaffold from which a person or object could fall more than:",
 "4 metres", ["2 metres", "3 metres", "6 metres"],
 "Scaffolding work where a person or thing could fall more than 4 metres must be done by a holder of a scaffolding high risk work licence."),
("You find a scaffold with no inspection tag. What should you do?",
 "Don't use it; report it", ["Use it if it looks sound", "Use it for light work only", "Fill in a tag yourself"],
 "A scaffold must be inspected by a competent person before first use and regularly after that, and the tag shows it has been. Without one, treat it as unsafe and tell your supervisor."),
("How should an incomplete scaffold be marked?",
 "Danger signs and access blocked", ["A green safe-to-use scaffold tag", "A rope across the top lift", "Nothing until it is finished"],
 "An incomplete scaffold must have danger tags or signs and its access points blocked so nobody climbs on before it is finished and inspected."),
("Who may remove a scaffold tie, brace or plank?",
 "A competent, authorised scaffolder", ["Any worker who needs to get past it", "The first trade on site that day", "Anyone holding a White Card"],
 "Removing parts of a scaffold can make it collapse, so only a competent person authorised to alter it, licensed if over 4 m, may do so. Ask for the change; do not do it yourself."),
("How high above the working surface should a guardrail's top rail be?",
 "900 mm to 1100 mm", ["500 mm to 700 mm", "1200 mm to 1500 mm", "Any height over 600 mm"],
 "The construction falls code of practice places the top rail between 900 mm and 1100 mm above the working surface, with a mid-rail and a toe board."),
("What is the main job of a toe board on a work platform?",
 "Stop objects falling off", ["Stop workers slipping", "Hold the guardrail up", "Mark where a plank ends"],
 "A toe board along the platform edge stops tools and materials being kicked or rolled off onto people below."),
("Work is being done overhead. What should be set up on the ground below?",
 "An exclusion zone", ["A smoking area", "A material stockpile", "A first aid station"],
 "An exclusion zone with barriers and signs keeps people out of the area where objects could fall. Catch nets, toe boards and tool lanyards help as well."),
("How can you stop hand tools falling when working at height?",
 "Attach them with lanyards", ["Rest them on the handrail", "Keep them near the edge", "Carry them in loose pockets"],
 "Tool lanyards tie tools to your belt or the structure so a dropped tool cannot fall onto someone below. A tool resting on a rail is easily knocked off."),
("How should an open hole or penetration in a floor be made safe?",
 "Covered, secured and marked", ["Taped off and left open", "Covered with loose plywood", "Marked with spray paint only"],
 "A cover must be strong enough to take the load, fixed so it cannot move and marked as a hole. A loose sheet of ply can slide or be lifted away."),
("Why is walking on skylights or brittle roof sheeting dangerous?",
 "It can break under you", ["It gets too hot in summer", "It holds rainwater", "It is slippery when dry"],
 "Brittle and fragile roofing, such as skylights and some sheeting, can shatter under a person's weight. Falls through roofs are a common cause of death in construction."),
("Which of these can cause an unplanned collapse?",
 "Overloading a floor or formwork", ["Stacking materials on firm ground", "Using a tagged, inspected scaffold", "Bracing walls during the work"],
 "Overloading floors, formwork or scaffolds, removing supports too early and undermining footings can all lead to collapse. Bracing and inspection help prevent it."),
("Who decides when formwork props can be removed?",
 "A competent person, per the plan", ["Any worker once it looks dry", "The first trade that needs the space", "The concrete truck driver"],
 "Props stay until the concrete has reached the strength the engineer specifies and a competent person approves removal. Concrete can look dry long before it can carry its load."),
("Before digging, how should you find out where underground services are?",
 "Lodge a Before You Dig enquiry", ["Dig a test hole with plant", "Ask the property's neighbours", "Check the council rates notice"],
 "Before You Dig Australia (formerly Dial Before You Dig) is a free service that sends plans of underground pipes and cables. The plans are a guide; services still need to be located on site."),
("Plans show a gas main near where you will dig. How should it be exposed?",
 "Carefully, by hand or vacuum", ["With a jackhammer", "With a road saw", "With the excavator bucket, slowly"],
 "Services are located by careful hand digging or vacuum excavation before machines dig near them. A bucket or jackhammer can rupture a gas main or cut a live cable."),
("Which of these protects workers from a trench wall collapsing?",
 "Shoring the trench walls", ["Working faster in the trench", "Wetting the trench floor", "Piling spoil at the edge"],
 "Shoring, battering or benching the walls stops a collapse. Spoil at the edge adds weight and makes collapse more likely."),
("Where should spoil from an excavation be placed?",
 "Well back from the edge", ["Right at the trench edge", "Inside the trench itself", "On the access ramp"],
 "Spoil kept back from the edge does not overload the trench wall or fall back in onto workers. Its distance should be set in the excavation plan."),
("Why should heavy plant be kept back from the edge of a trench?",
 "Its weight can collapse the wall", ["It may block access to the trench", "Its noise drowns out a spotter", "It scratches the trench shoring"],
 "The weight and vibration of plant near the edge adds load to the trench wall and can cause it to cave in on workers below."),
("How should workers get in and out of a deep trench?",
 "By a secured ladder or ramp", ["By climbing down the shoring", "By jumping down", "In the excavator bucket"],
 "A secured ladder, stairway or ramp gives a safe way in and out, including in an emergency. Riding in a bucket and climbing shoring both cause serious injuries."),
]
